      "text": "## Maps as Compressed Worldview"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#002",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:cartography:00-overview, git-history:cartography:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "cartography/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "cartography/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:cartography:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cartography",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "cartography",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Cartography - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A map is not a picture of the world. It is an argument about the world — which features matter, how large things are relative to each other, what belongs at the center, and what gets omitted entirely. Every cartographic choice encodes a perspective. Understanding cartography means understanding that compression is always lossy, and the loss is never neutral."
    },
